 ('hnagagroup', ('hnagagroup8', 'wnagakc', 'wnaga9'), 'max', '2:3', "a tall Thai temple naga sculpture about 2.1 metres tall and 1.1 wide, standing on the ground: a FIVE-headed naga king. Five slender rearing naga necks in antique bronze-gold fan out side by side like the fingers of an open hand, the middle one tallest and upright, the two outer ones leaning far out to the left and to the right; each neck is banded down its front with broad belly scales, and each ends in a crested naga head with a tall pointed flame crest and an open mouth with small white fangs and a red tongue. Where the five necks join, they rise out of a ring of large pink lotus petals with pale edges, like an open lotus flower. Under the lotus the naga's own body is wound round and round in a tall neat stack of about twelve thick rope-like coils of pale straw-brown, one directly above another like a coiled rope, forming a round column about 55 centimetres across and 1.2 metres tall that stands on the ground. In front of the column, at its right side, stands a second, lower stack of five darker brown coils, and from it rise two smaller nagas with long smooth glossy RED bodies that curve upward in S shapes, each ending in a small dark bronze crested head. THE REFERENCES: the first is a ragged cut-out of the real sculpture, small and soft, with the grey bars of another object (an electric fan on a tripod) across it: IGNORE the grey bars and draw no fan. The second and third are other naga statues: use them only for how the heads, crests, open mouths, belly scales and fanned necks of such nagas are made, in the bronze-gold and straw-brown of the first reference, NOT in their colours."),
